course: everyday
lesson: 88
title: Your body and how you look
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every person looks different: tall or short, curly or straight hair, freckles, glasses, braces, different skin colours and body shapes; there is no single right way to look.
explain: Your worth is who you are and what you do, like being kind, brave, funny or a good friend, not how you look.
explain: Pictures in adverts and online are often edited, so real people do not look like them.
explain: A girl in Austin is teased about her freckles; she remembers that her freckles are just part of her, tells her teacher when it keeps happening, and the teasing stops.
fact: Everybody looks different, and that is normal.
fact: Your worth does not depend on your looks.
fact: Someone being unkind about your looks says more about them than about you.
fact: If someone keeps being unkind about how you look, tell a trusted adult.
fact: Pictures in adverts and on social media are often edited and filtered.
fact: Your body is for doing things, like running, laughing, hugging and learning, not only for looking at.
fact: Saying kind things about yourself, and to yourself, helps you feel better.
fact: We should never make fun of how other people look either.
exercise: Someone at school says your hair looks silly. What is a good thing to remember? | Your worth is not your looks | You must change your hair at once | They must be right about you | You should say something mean back
exercise: The same girl is unkind about how you look every day. What should you do? | Tell a trusted adult | Keep it a secret for ever | Stop coming to school | Hide your face under a hood
exercise: Which is true about how people look? | Everybody looks different, and that is normal | Everyone should try hard to look the same as everyone else | Only tall people look right | People with glasses look wrong
exercise: Why do people in adverts often look perfect? | The pictures are often edited | They never eat any food | They are a different kind of human | Cameras always show the truth
exercise: What makes a person a good friend? | Being kind, honest and fun to be with | Having the newest trainers and the coolest phone | Being the tallest in the class | Having very straight teeth
exercise: What is your body really for? | Doing things like running, laughing and learning | Only being looked at and judged by other people | Matching the people in adverts | Winning prizes for looks
exercise: You catch yourself thinking «I look awful». What helps? | Saying something kind to yourself | Looking in the mirror for an hour | Comparing yourself with a film star | Asking strangers what they think
exercise: A new boy has a birthmark on his face. How should you treat him? | Like anyone else, kindly and without staring | Point it out to everyone in the class so they know | Ask him to cover it up | Keep away from him
exercise: What can it mean when someone is mean about others' looks? | It says more about them than about you | They are always telling the truth about how you look | They are the best judge of looks | You must agree with them
exercise: Your friend is sad because she is the shortest in the class. What could you say? | Everyone looks different and you are great as you are | You will look better when you grow | Wear tall shoes so that no one in the class notices how short you are | Yes, it is a big problem
exercise: Which is the best reason to feel proud of yourself? | Something you did, like helping or trying hard | Your nose being a certain shape that people like | Your hair being a certain colour | Having expensive clothes
exercise: A classmate makes a joke about another girl's weight. What is right? | Do not join in, and tell an adult if it goes on | Laugh along with everyone so that you fit in with the group | Add your own joke | Tell the girl she should change
test: A boy in Cardiff wears braces and someone calls him «metal mouth» every day. What should he do? | Tell a trusted adult | Take the braces off himself | Stop smiling for ever | Call the other boy names
test: A girl compares herself with a photo of a model online and feels bad. What should she know? | Such photos are often edited and filtered | Everyone should look like that photo | She must change her body to look like it | The photo shows a normal person on a normal day
test: Which thought shows a healthy way to see yourself? | I am kind and I try hard, and I look like me | I am only good if I look like my best friend does | I must be the best looking to be liked | My looks are the most important thing
test: Two friends look very different. What is true? | Both look normal, because everyone is different | One of them must look wrong | Only the taller one looks right | They must try hard to look the same as each other
test: Why should you not make fun of how someone looks? | It hurts them, and everyone looks different | Only because a teacher might hear you and you could get into trouble | It is fine if you whisper it | It is fine if they laugh too
test: A friend says, «Nobody will like me because of my freckles.» What is the best answer? | People like you for who you are | Yes, freckles are a real problem for making friends | Cover them with paint | Stay out of the sun for ever
test: What does a strong, healthy body help you do? | Run, play, learn and enjoy life | Look like the people in adverts and on TV | Win every argument | Never feel tired at all
test: Why does it help to tell an adult when looks-teasing keeps happening? | They can help stop it and support you | They will tell you to change your looks | They will laugh at the joke too | Adults cannot do anything about it
test: that girl on insta's got perfect skin, mine's rubbish 😞 | Pictures online are often edited and filtered | Her skin really is perfect all day | Your skin is bad if it is not like hers | Everyone online looks like that for real, all the time
test: he keeps saying my ears r massive every day | Tell a trusted adult | Hide your ears with a hat forever | Say something mean about him back | Believe him, he must be right
test: im not pretty so no ones gonna like me | Your worth does not depend on your looks | Only pretty people have friends | You are right, looks matter most | Get new clothes and then they will all like you
test: can we laugh at his big glasses he cant hear us | No, never make fun of how people look | Yes, it's fine if he cannot hear you | Yes, glasses are funny | Yes, if everyone else is laughing
explain: A girl in California skipped meals because a video said she should look thinner; she felt dizzy at school, could not think in tests and was taken to a doctor.
explain: A boy in Leeds was teased about his ears and said nothing to anyone; for months he hid in the library at lunch until a teacher noticed and helped.
fact: Many photos and videos online are edited or filtered, so they show bodies that do not really look like that.
fact: Not eating enough to change how you look can make you ill, tired and unable to concentrate.
test: influencer says skip meals to get skinny, gonna try | No, skipping meals makes you ill and tired, talk to an adult | Yes, it's a safe trick | Yes, if you drink lots of fizzy drinks | Only skip breakfast and lunch
test: everyone online looks perfect n i dont | Many pictures are edited, real bodies look different | Real people all look like that | You must change to look like them | Only you look different
test: kids keep teasing me bout my ears, gonna keep it quiet | Tell a trusted adult, teasing can be stopped | Keep quiet, it'll stop by itself | Tease them back meaner | Hide from everyone at lunch
test: did u see that influencer's legs, my sister says she's had like 10 surgeries, mine'll never look like that | Online photos are often edited and filtered, so do not compare your real body to them | Her photos are real, so you should diet to look like her | Everyone can look like that with enough effort | The surgery story must be true because it is online
test: heard someone in Year 6 stopped eating lunch to get thinner n fainted in PE, is that a thing | Not eating enough to change how you look makes you ill and tired, so tell a trusted adult | Skipping meals is a safe way to change how you look | Fainting means the diet is working well | It only matters if you skip more than a week
test: the group chat's rating everyone's faces out of 10, they want me to rate Ella's | Do not rate her; it is unkind, and making fun of looks hurts people | Rate her kindly, a 7 is not mean | Rate her, but only if she is not in the chat | Rate her low so she tries harder next time
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the value of a person? | A person's worth does not depend upon their physical appearance | A person's worth is determined principally by their height and weight | A person's worth depends upon the number of photographs taken of them | A person's worth increases in proportion to their resemblance to models
exam: Identify the principal reason for which images in advertisements and on social media ought not to serve as a standard for one's own body. | Such images are frequently edited and filtered, and do not show bodies as they really are | Such images are invariably accurate, but depict persons of unusually advanced age | Such images are prohibited by law from being altered in any manner whatsoever | Such images depict the average body, which nobody should desire to resemble
exam: Pursuant to repeated unkind remarks concerning a child's appearance, select the course of action consistent with the established facts. | The child informs a trusted adult of the remarks | The child alters their appearance in order to stop them | The child responds with unkind remarks of their own | The child conceals the matter from all adults indefinitely
exam: In respect of the deliberate restriction of food in order to alter one's appearance, which statement is correct? | It may render a person ill and tired and impair their concentration | It is a healthy habit which improves concentration at school | It has no effect upon the body other than upon its appearance | It is recommended for growing children before the summer holidays
